      - display: "D&C 59:9–12"
        url: "dc-testament/dc/59?id=p9#p9"

    practice: |
      <h4>Why God Asks Us to Rest</h4>
      <p>Even the immortal Creator rested from His labors on the seventh day and hallowed it (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/ex/20?id=p11#p11" target="_blank">Exodus 20:11</a>). If God Himself set apart a day for rest, how much more do His mortal children need it? In Hebrew the word is <em>shabbāth</em> — rest. It is one of the Ten Commandments, and it has never been revoked.</p>
      <p>After the resurrection of Christ on Sunday — the first day of the week — Christians began observing the Lord's Day on Sunday rather than Saturday. Modern revelation confirms this pattern: we are to go to the house of prayer, offer up our sacraments, rest from our labors, and pay our devotions unto the Most High (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/dc-testament/dc/59?id=p9#p9" target="_blank">D&amp;C 59:9–12</a>). The Sabbath is not merely a pause in the schedule. It is a day consecrated to God.</p>

    blesses: |
      <h4>The Day That Resets Everything</h4>
      <p>For years I treated Sunday as the day I recovered from Saturday. I slept in, scrolled through my phone, and told myself I was "resting." But I was not renewed — I was just idle. When I finally began treating the Sabbath as something set apart rather than leftover, the difference was immediate. The week had an anchor. I walked into Monday carrying something I had not earned — a quiet steadiness that came from having stopped long enough to remember whose I was.</p>
      <p>The Sabbath has taught me that rest is not the absence of activity. It is the presence of God in the pause. He does not need my productivity on Sunday. He wants my attention.</p>
      <p class="bridge-text">If your weeks feel like an unbroken blur of obligation, the Sabbath may be the reset you did not know you needed. The commitment below offers one way to begin setting the day apart.</p>

    commitments:
      - text: "I will attend sacrament meeting this Sunday and treat the full day as consecrated to the Lord."
        tier: "covenant"
      - text: "I will set aside one hour this Sunday for worship, scripture reading, or prayer — free from screens and errands."
        tier: "seeker"
      - text: "I will choose one day this week to deliberately slow down and reflect on what matters most to me."
        tier: "explore"

    reflection: "What would change in your week if one full day truly belonged to God?"

  # ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  # CARD 2: Making the Sabbath a Delight
  # ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  - title: "Making the Sabbath a Delight"
    scriptures:
      - display: "Isaiah 58:13–14"
        url: "ot/isa/58?id=p13#p13"
      - display: "Mark 2:27"
        url: "nt/mark/2?id=p27#p27"

    practice: |
      <h4>Holy, Not Heavy</h4>
      <p>Jesus taught that the Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/nt/mark/2?id=p27#p27" target="_blank">Mark 2:27</a>). The Sabbath is not a day of rigid prohibitions designed to burden us. It is a gift — a weekly invitation to step out of the world's demands and into God's presence. The prophet Isaiah called it a delight and promised that those who honor it would find joy in the Lord (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/isa/58?id=p13#p13" target="_blank">Isaiah 58:13–14</a>).</p>
      <p>Latter-day Saints keep the Sabbath by gathering for worship, partaking of the sacrament, and spending the day in activities that draw us closer to God and to each other — visiting family, writing in journals, studying the scriptures, serving those in need, and simply being still. We set aside work, shopping, and entertainment not because these things are evil, but because one day a week we choose something higher. Each family prayerfully decides what honors the day.</p>

    blesses: |
      <h4>The Delight I Did Not Expect</h4>
      <p>I expected the Sabbath to feel restrictive. I grew up in a culture that prizes productivity, and the idea of a whole day where I was not supposed to "get things done" felt like a loss. But what I found was the opposite. When I stopped filling Sunday with errands and entertainment, space opened up — space I did not know I needed. Conversations with my family went deeper. Prayers went longer without me noticing. I read things I would never have made time for. The delight Isaiah promised was not dramatic. It was quiet and real.</p>
      <p>The Sabbath has become the day I look forward to most — not because nothing happens, but because what happens on that day is entirely different from the rest of the week.</p>
      <p class="bridge-text">You do not have to observe the Sabbath perfectly to begin experiencing its blessings. Start with what feels honest, and let the day teach you.</p>

    commitments:
      - text: "I will plan my Sabbath intentionally this week — choosing activities that draw me toward God and away from worldly distractions."
        tier: "covenant"
      - text: "I will replace one routine Sunday activity (shopping, chores, or entertainment) with time spent in reflection, reading, or family connection."
        tier: "seeker"
      - text: "I will try spending thirty minutes this week in a quiet activity — no phone, no screen — and notice how it feels."
        tier: "explore"

    reflection: "What would your Sundays look like if you designed them around renewal instead of recovery?"

  # ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  # CARD 3: A Sign Between God and His People
  # ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  - title: "A Sign Between God and His People"
    scriptures:
      - display: "Exodus 31:13"
        url: "ot/ex/31?id=p13#p13"
      - display: "Mosiah 18:9"
        url: "bofm/mosiah/18?id=p9#p9"

    practice: |
      <h4>The Sabbath as Covenant Marker</h4>
      <p>God told ancient Israel that the Sabbath was a sign between Him and His people — a visible declaration that they belonged to Him and that He was the Lord who sanctified them (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/ot/ex/31?id=p13#p13" target="_blank">Exodus 31:13</a>). The Sabbath is not merely a good habit. It is a covenant act — an outward expression of an inward devotion.</p>
      <p>For Latter-day Saints the center of Sabbath worship is the sacrament, the ordinance in which we partake of bread and water in remembrance of the body and blood of Christ. In doing so we witness before God that we are willing to take upon us the name of His Son, always remember Him, and keep His commandments (<a class="scripture-link" href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/bofm/moro/4?id=p3#p3" target="_blank">Moroni 4:3</a>). This weekly renewal of baptismal covenants is the heartbeat of Sabbath observance.</p>

    blesses: |
      <h4>A Weekly Homecoming</h4>
      <p>There have been Sundays when I arrived at church distracted, burdened, or spiritually numb. More than once I wondered why I was there at all. And then the sacrament came. The prayers were spoken. The bread was broken. And something in me remembered — not just what Christ did, but who I had promised to become. That weekly return to the covenant table has rescued me more times than I can count. It does not erase the hard week behind me, but it gives me clean ground to stand on for the week ahead.</p>
      <p>The Sabbath is God's weekly homecoming for His children. No matter how far we have wandered during the week, He sets the table again and waits for us to come.</p>
      <p class="bridge-text">Whether you have kept the Sabbath your whole life or have never tried, the invitation is always the same — come, and see what God does with a day you give to Him.</p>

    commitments:
      - text: "I will prepare for the sacrament each week with prayer and self-examination, making it the spiritual center of my Sabbath."
        tier: "covenant"
      - text: "I will attend a worship service this Sunday with the intention of being spiritually present, not just physically there."
        tier: "seeker"
      - text: "I will spend a few minutes this week thinking about whether there is something sacred I want to return to — a practice, a community, or a conversation with God."
        tier: "explore"

    reflection: "What does it mean to you to belong to something larger than yourself?"
